  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\na\\times (3a-2b)=a\\times (3a)-a\\times (2b)\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\na\\times (3a)-a\\times (2b)=3(a\\times a)-2(a\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Then, we use the anticommutativity property and the fact that $a\\times a=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\times a)-2(a\\times b) & =3(0)-2(-b\\times a) \\\\  & =2(b\\times a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $b\\times a$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(b\\times a) & =2\u27e86,0,-3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e812,0,-6\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
